{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310776,"nonce":"dxHFB/Fx1QihdQ2udlfQMt2d1m8=","title":"CORRECTED. At HEAD the per-token leak is ~0.16 MB/token (1.70M live allocs / 571 MB), NOT the 1.7 MB/token I first reported -- that ...","message":"I profiled `/tmp/nue_bench_chat.rzb` (mtime Aug 9 14:42) and reported a\n1.7 MB/token leak with `haxe_bytes_alloc` at 12,608,714 calls and\n`haxe_box_bool_ptr` at 11,648,186. **All of it was pre-1770a9ee code** (that\ncommit landed Aug 10 09:55 and hoists the band scratch out of the closure).\nThe mistral gate itself was FINE — it uses a DIFFERENT bundle,\n`/tmp/nue_bench_mistral.rzb`, and rebuilds it correctly (`bench_stale` does\nwatch `$REPO/nue/nue`). I picked the wrong .rzb out of habit.\n**RULE: before any profiling run, `stat` the .rzb against the .hx you care\nabout. `llama-chat/gate.sh` -\u003e /tmp/nue_bench_chat.rzb;\n`mistral/gate.sh` -\u003e /tmp/nue_bench_mistral.rzb; they are NOT the same file.**\nA row labelled with the current commit says nothing about the bundle's age —\nthe label comes from git, not from the artifact.\n\n`footprint -p` during generation: 4405 -\u003e 4500 MB over 36s @ 16.1 tok/s\n= **~2.6 MB/s = ~0.16 MB per token** (600 tokens ~= 95 MB). Still monotonic,\nstill never turns over, but ~10x smaller than the stale reading. An 8k-token\nchat costs ~1.3 GB — worth fixing, not an emergency.\n`heap`: **1,703,695 live allocations / 571 MB** (was 26.4M / 1.45 GB stale).\n\n| bytes | calls | site |\n|---|---|---|\n| 360.1 MB | **4,114** | `haxe_bytes_alloc` — avg 87 KB, i.e. real buffers, NOT churn |\n| 39.5 MB | 691,610 | `???` JIT-allocated, unattributable |\n| 19.5 MB | 255,504 | `haxe_array_push` |\n| 13.0 MB | 213,822 | `rayzor_tensor_shape` |\n| 32.4 MB | 113 | `realloc` |\n\n`haxe_bytes_alloc` 12.6M -\u003e 4,114 confirms 1770a9ee end-to-end (~3000x).\n`haxe_box_bool_ptr` does not appear at HEAD at all — the \"29k boxed bools per\ntoken\" was pre-fix code too. Do NOT go hunting a Bool-boxing compiler bug on\nthe strength of that; there is no evidence for one at HEAD.\n\n`Q4Matmul.dequantQ4KToF16` (Bytes.alloc(68)) and `narrowToF16` (Bytes.alloc(4))\nstill allocated INSIDE their `rowFn` band closures at HEAD — one malloc per\nCLAIMED CHUNK, escaping via `.address()` so insert_free emits nothing.\nPrefill-only (gated `batch \u003e= amxMinBatch()`, default 128), ~12k allocations\nper prefill. Fixed with the 1770a9ee shape: hoist per-worker, slice by `node`,\nfree after the synchronous dispatch — with a 128-byte (cache-line) stride,\nbecause these slots are written on every conversion and a tight stride would\nfalse-share. Verified: prefill output still coherent, 45.88 tok/s (no\nregression), footprint unchanged at 4870 MB.\n\n- `malloc_history $PID -callTree -invert`, then rank the DEPTH-2 children\n  (`indent == 6`, lines `      + N (size) name`). Depth-1 is just\n  `_malloc_zone_malloc_*` and tells you nothing.\n- JIT frames are `??? (in \u003cunknown binary\u003e)` — you get the Rust allocator name\n  and the THREAD ORIGIN, never the Haxe function. The worker/main split is the\n  most useful signal it gives you (a per-claim alloc shows ~7/8 on workers).\n- `MallocStackLogging=1` must be in the run's env at launch.\n- Keep the process ALIVE long enough to sample — stack logging slows the run,\n  so size MAX_TOKENS generously or `heap`/`malloc_history` return nothing.\n- macOS RSS understates; use `footprint -p PID` (phys_footprint), unprivileged.\n- ALWAYS state the token count next to a footprint number: the same build reads\n  4405 MB early and 4500 MB late in one run.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_anonymous_heap_attributed` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}